  - [1mConsole logging only[0m — enrich the existing [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m ([38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m) with the ID. No new dependency, no data leaves the
    browser, useful only when someone has devtools open. Genuinely sufficient if "track" means "I want to see it while
    debugging."
  - [1mA small internal event module[0m — a [38;5;153mtrackLoginEvent(userId, ...)[39m function you own, which today writes to the console
    and later can post somewhere. Costs one small file and defers the destination decision without blocking it. Zero
    third-party code.
  - [1mA third-party analytics SDK[0m (Segment, PostHog, GA) — you get dashboards and retention for free, and you take on a
    script that reads your page, a vendor relationship, and a consent/GDPR obligation, since login events tied to a user
    ID are personal data.
  - [1mPost to your own backend[0m — an endpoint you control receives the event. No vendor, no consent surprise, full control
    of retention; but it's backend work that doesn't exist yet.

  [1mMy recommendation: the second.[0m It's the YAGNI-correct answer — you asked to track who logged in, and a function with
  the right signature that currently logs to console gives you exactly that, plus one obvious place to change when you
  know where the data should go. Pulling in an analytics SDK now commits you to a vendor and a consent flow before
  you've established what question you're trying to answer with the data.

  Cheap to change afterward: the destination inside that function. Expensive: an SDK, once it's loaded on every page and
  other code depends on its API.
